\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  peso firms after cenbank halts  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 203 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2008\par 15:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Sept 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso rose 0.7 percent in early Tuesday trade, pulling back from a 3-year low hit a day earlier, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called an early halt to a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The peso <CHILJ> <CLP=CL> was trading at 547.80/548.30 per dollar at 8:30 am (1230 GMT) after closing 2.23 percent lower on Monday at 551.50/552.00.\par \par \uc2 The peso skidded on Monday to a three-year low as prices for copper -- Chile's main export -- fell below $3 a pound and investors worried that a global financial crisis could deepen.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $8 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan had seen the peso depreciate 22 percent since April. The bank said it was ending the program because of international financial turmoil.\par \par \uc2 The program, under which the bank bought $50 million worth of greenbacks daily, was 70 percent complete. It was originally introduced in April to reverse a sharp appreciation of the peso earlier in the year. (Reporting by Maria Jose Latorre; Editing by Andrea Ricci)\par \par \uc2 CHILE-PESO/|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080930e49u0010s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
