\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Peso Ends Weaker Despite End Of Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolina Pica   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2008\par 20:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--The Chilean peso closed slightly weaker against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, falling to a fresh three-year low, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement that it ended its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program.\par \par \uc2 A sharp tumble in spot copper prices on the London Metal Exchange and uncertainty over the outcome of the U.S. government's $700 billion financial bailout plan, which the U.S. House of Representatives rejected Monday afternoon, pulled the peso down, trader said.\par \par \uc2 The peso ended the session at CLP552.00 to the dollar, compared with CLP551.40 at Monday's close. The peso moved in a broad range of CLP548.00 to CLP556.30 during the session.\par \par \uc2 State copper commission Cochilco reported that LME spot copper fell 2.6% from Monday's close to a year-to-date low of $2.91161 a pound. As Chile is the world's largest copper producer, market participants and analysts look at copper prices to gauge the general health of the economy. Cochilco estimates the mining industry represents 22% of the country's gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Late Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it ended its $8 billion program to beef up its foreign reserves through the end of the year. The bank, which had been making daily $50 million purchases on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, said it ended the purchases in light of international market conditions.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program in mid-April, after the peso firmed to 10-year highs in March as a result of sky-high copper prices. It began daily $50 million purchases on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with the aim of building up foreign reserves by $8 billion at the end of 2008.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the bank's daily dollar purchases came under fire, as some analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program had weakened the peso too much, which in turn caused inflation to rise.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the bank on Monday said that to ease the normal functioning of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and interbank dollar liquidity it would resume its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap tender offers starting Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Before trading ended Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered local banks up to $500 million in dollar swaps to increase greenback liquidity on the local market. The swaps were offered at a premium of 3.49% over the London Interbank Offered Rate, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on its Web site.\par \par \uc2 The total amount banks tendered reached 214.36 billion Chilean pesos at a fixed exchange rate of CLP552.47 to the dollar, or $389 million.\par \par \uc2 The peso could test a significant support level at CLP558 in coming sessions if copper prices continue to tumble and markets remain uncertain as to how the U.S. government will navigate the ratification of its bailout plan through congress, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 On the bond market, Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds ended considerably lower on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to end its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program ahead of schedule. The program included the neutralization of the daily dollar purchases through auctions of five- and 10-year bond. These auctions also ended Monday.\par \par \uc2 In addition, a higher September inflation outlook, in light of current crude oil prices, also brought yields down. Chile is a net crude importer.\par \par \uc2 Bonds are indexed for inflation so that a higher inflation outlook makes them a more attractive investment.\par \par \uc2 The yield on inflation-indexed five-year BCUs ended at 3.10%, down from 3.28% Tuesday, while the yield on the 10-year BCUs ended at 3.20%, down from 3.34% Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Carolina Pica, Dow Jones Newswires; 56-2-820-4244; carolina.pica@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Patricia San Juan contributed to this report.) [ 30-09-08 1802GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1170 1844   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080930e49u0012q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
