\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  cenbank: Peso  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  still justified\b0\par\par\uc2 229 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2008\par 16:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Sept 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jose De Gregorio on Friday said the international financial risks that led to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April still exist.\par \par \uc2 "The financial risks have not calmed," De Gregorio told a seminar of Chile's top businessmen.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched an $8 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program in April aimed at reverting the peso's sharp appreciation earlier in the year and at boosting foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the peso was trading at 526.70 pesos per dollar in late morning trade.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar ended last year at 497.80 pesos and then slumped to 430 pesos in March, the peso's strongest level in over a decade, by which time it had appreciated 15 percent for the year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 The peso appreciated on strong trade surpluses fueled by high world commodities prices, particularly copper, and by robust economic growth, but exporters complained the cheap U.S. dollar hurt profits and their ability to compete in world markets.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday that it bought $50 million worth of dollars at an average price of 525.94 pesos each. (Reporting by Rodrigo Martinez; writing by Pav Jordan; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CHILE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CENBANK/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokum