\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD: Emerging Mkt  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Steal Bonds' Thunder\b0\par\par\uc2 By Min Zeng   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 915 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2008\par 19:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Bond investors, be aware: if you want to boost returns in emerging markets, stay away from government debt and go for pure plays in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Surging food, energy and raw material prices have spurred rising inflation worldwide, forcing central banks from Asia and Europe to Latin America to raise interest rates, while allowing local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate to keep price pressures from running out of control.\par \par \uc2 The policies have prompted a switch in investment strategies as rising interest rates push up bond yields, reducing the value of fixed income securities. Bond prices fall when yields rise. Bonds are also less attractive amid concerns over inflation, which eats into their fixed interest payments over time.\par \par \uc2 "A strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably is one of the more popular policy tools central banks are going to utilize, along with higher interest rates, over the next year or two," said Nick Chamie, head of emerging markets at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 Chamie said some of the best destinations for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bets are Brazil, Russia, Mexico and Colombia where economic fundamentals are sound, central banks are hawkish and expected to rapidly tackle inflation. On the other hand, he recommended investors shun \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in South Africa, South Korea, India and Argentina.\par \par \uc2 Brazil stands out as a popular choice. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has tightened interest rates by 100 basis points this year, pushing up its key rate to 12.25%, among the highest in the world. Brazil began to hike rates in April, after being on hold since September.\par \par \uc2 The rate increases came as inflation in May stood at 5.58%, well above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target of 4.5%. There is growing concern that food-led price pressures will spill over into wages and other sectors if left unattended.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil's real is still attractive," said Igor Arsenin, an emerging-market fixed-income strategist at \uc2 Credit Suisse Group\uc2  in New York. "I expect interest rates to rise significantly further in Brazil."\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Rising Ruble \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Arsenin said he expected Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to boost the key rate to 14.25% by the end of the year and the Brazil real, which traded at BRL1.6151 to the dollar Monday, to strengthen below BRL1.6 against the dollar over the same period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real has risen 9.35% against the dollar so far this year, while the Mexican peso, Russian ruble and the Colombian peso have gained 5.96%, 4.05%, and 18.1%, respectively, over the same period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday, Brazil's 10-year government debt yielded 5.578%, higher than the 4.16% on offer on 10-year Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dario Pedrajo, who manages $100 million at Kapax Investment Advisers LLC in Miami, targeted the Russian ruble, whose value has been artificially held back by the local government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the Russian government shifting its attention to the fight against inflation, it may allow greater appreciation in the ruble against the dollar, making this a good time to bet on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's gains, he said. The ruble was trading at RUB23.6607 to the dollar Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pedrajo also noted that the Russian ruble is a better bet than the Chinese yuan because he doesn't expect a significant appreciation in the yuan in the foreseeable future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan, which traded at CNY6.8740 to the dollar Monday, has gained 5.05% this year and has risen more than 20% since a dollar peg was removed in July 2005.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Risks \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bets aren't without risks - as seen in the past month with the dollar's rebound against its major rivals amid the shifting interest rate outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market is now betting on the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates by the end of the year, a sharp reversal from several months ago when rate futures markets were discounting additional rate cuts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bets on higher rates, however, were moderated last week after The Wall Street Journal and the Financial Times reported that Fed officials were divided on tightening and a rate hike may not be a sure thing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The swings in Fed rate expectations fueled fluctuations in bonds and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, adding to the risks of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors need to be aware of the fact that markets remain extremely nervous," said Chamie. "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bets need to be a very selective process."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors also need to be mindful that some emerging market governments may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to limit the appreciation of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to protect their exporters. Rising \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  make local products less competitive in international markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Latin America, governments in Chile and Colombia are looking to contain gains in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. Since April, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Chile\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been buying $50 million a day to reverse the Chilean peso's appreciation. Colombia Friday said it plans to buy $20 million a day in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Min Zeng writes about Treasurys markets for Dow Jones Newswires.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Min Zeng, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; min.zeng@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments [ 23-06-08 1724GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 70718   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080623e46n000qm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
