\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Chile market expects  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  rate hike of 25 bps\b0\par\par\uc2 533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2008\par 20:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * What: Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  benchmark rate decision\par \par \uc2 * When: 6 p.m. (2200 GMT) Tuesday, June 10\par \par \uc2 * Forecast: Most expect rate hike to 6.50 percent\par \par \uc2 By Maria Jose Latorre\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, June 9 (Reuters) - A Reuters poll on Monday showed a majority of market watchers expect Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise its key interest rate a quarter of a percentage point at its monthly monetary policy meeting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Of 26 traders and analysts polled, 21 said they expected the bank to raise the monetary policy rate <CLTPM=> to 6.50 percent in the first rate hike since January, on recent higher-than-expected inflation and growth data.\par \par \uc2 May inflation of 1.2 percent and April economic growth of 4.8 percent released last week exceeded market expectations and reinforced views the bank might raise rates to control inflation, a move it had shunned given a weaker dollar and cooling growth.\par \par \uc2 "We think the bank is going to raise the rate 25 basis points because of the positive surprise in growth and the negative surprise in inflation. On top of that, trade balance data is showing that consumer demand is healthy, that it's strong enough to pull the wagon," said Bertrand Delgado, an economist with IDEAglobal in New York.\par \par \uc2 June trade figures released on Monday showed Chile's consumer imports up about 25 percent and foreign investment surging near 50 percent above the same month last year.\par \par \uc2 DOLLAR EXCHANGE RATE\par \par \uc2 Another factor in Chile's monetary policy has been a weaker dollar, which started the year at about 498 pesos and then tumbled to 430 pesos, leading \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to institute an $8 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program in April to curb the appreciation of the peso <CLP=>.\par \par \uc2 The peso's relative strength against the dollar left the bank reluctant to raise interest rates, which would strengthen the peso further, but on Monday the greenback was trading above 480 pesos per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Not all agreed that the bank should raise the rate.\par \par \uc2 "There's still the possibility they could hold the rate. The main factors behind rising inflation are external, such as oil prices, and they'll continue to rise," one trader said.\par \par \uc2 Chile, which imports nearly all of the fuel it consumes, has seen energy costs rocket amid short supplies of natural gas from Argentina and lower hydroelectric reservoir levels after last year's scant rainfall.\par \par \uc2 Last year annual inflation soared to 7.8 percent from only 2.6 percent in 2006, leading the bank to hike rates five times between July 2007 and January 2008.\par \par \uc2 According to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll released on Monday, analysts predicted annual inflation at 5.5 percent, up from the prior estimate of 4.7 percent, and above the bank's 12- to 24-month target rate of 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth for 2008 was seen at 4 percent in the survey, lower than the rate of 5.1 percent posted last year. (Reporting by Maria Jose Latorre and Lisa Yulkowski; Editing by James Dalgleish; )\par \par \uc2 CHILE-ECONOMY/RATES (POLL)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|C|GRO|MTL|SOF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080612e469001xc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
