\par\par \b \uc2 Chile stocks close lower after rally, peso advances\b0\par\par\uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2008\par 19:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details, closing stock figures)\par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, April 23 (Reuters) - Chilean stocks ended lower on Wednesday, as investors took post-rally profits and waited for quarterly corporate earnings reports due from local companies this week.\par \par \uc2 The peso advanced for a second day against the flagging dollar despite a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid to curb the appreciation of the peso by purchasing $50 million per day on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The all-market IGPA index <.IGPA> slipped 0.74 percent to 14,045.24 points, while the blue-chip IPSA <.IPSA> shed 0.86 percent to 3,017.54. The blue chip index rose as gains in forestry stocks offset losses that had sent the index down 2 percent in early trade.\par \par \uc2 Chilean stock indexes had risen nearly 5 percent in a seven-day rally that ended on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "Today more than anything there was profit taking, which after a 5 percent gain was to be expected," said Pamela Auszenker, an analyst with the BCI brokerage. "Oil prices are also an issue, especially with Chile's current energy shortfall and its impact on costs and profit margins."\par \par \uc2 Chile imports nearly all the fuel it consumes, and energy prices have surged as cuts in supplies of Argentine natural gas and lower hydroelectric reservoir levels force generators to burn costlier, dirtier diesel.\par \par \uc2 Auszenker also said investors were taking positions according to projected local corporate results that will begin to be released on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Declines on Chile's blue-chip index were led by electric utilities and chemical company SQM, with 24 of 40 blue-chip issues closing lower.\par \par \uc2 Stock in Soquimich , the world's largest lithium and iodine producer, which had risen 20 percent during the recent rally, was Wednesday's blue-chip loss leader, tumbling 5.11 percent.\par \par \uc2 Endesa Spain regional energy group \uc2 Enersis\uc2  fell 1.34 percent, while generating subsidiary \uc2 Endesa Chile\uc2  retreated 1.73 percent.\par \par \uc2 Declines among retailers were led by department store chain Ripley , down 3.37 percent.\par \par \uc2 Leading wood pulp producers CMPC and Copec , helped offset blue-chip losses with late-session advances of 0.84 percent and 1.5 percent, respectively.\par \par \uc2 The peso <CHILJ> <CLP=CL> rose 0.94 percent to close at 444.80/445.30 pesos per dollar, compared with Tuesday's close at 449.00/449.50.\par \par \uc2 "I think the market got realistic after an initial over-reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Investors realized that the dollar's appreciation was not sustainable and began taking profits," one trader said.\par \par \uc2 It was the second sharp gain for the peso since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  implemented a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan that includes buying an additional $8 billion on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the remainder of the year.\par \par \uc2 The bank began buying $50 million per day on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on April 14 in the first phase of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the world's biggest copper exporter, and rising prices for the red metal have supported the peso's strength by helping push trade surpluses to record levels. (Reporting by Froilan Romero and Lisa Yulkowski; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHILE/ (UPDATE 2)|LA