\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Chile central bank intervention \highlight\uc2  impact seen limited\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Yulkowski   \par \uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2008\par 22:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, April 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has followed in Mexico and Brazil's footsteps by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the strength of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the dollar, but the move is seen having limited impact and stoking inflation.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar ended last year at 497.80 pesos and then slumped to 430 pesos in March, the peso's strongest level in over a decade, by which time it had appreciated 15 percent for the year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso has been appreciating on strong trade surpluses fueled by high world commodities prices, particularly copper, and by robust economic growth. But exporters have been complaining the cheap U.S. dollar hurts their profits and their ability to be compete in world markets.\par \par \uc2 After its monetary policy meeting last Thursday, the bank surprised markets with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program that included buying an additional $8 billion worth of reserves in dollars by the year-end to erode the strength of the peso.\par \par \uc2 "The end result is that it probably does slow down domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, but it doesn't completely curtail it. It won't affect market fluctuations day-to-day," said Greg Anderson, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with ABN AMRO in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 The day following the announcement, the peso <CHILJ> <CLP=CL> fell 2.86 percent. The bank implemented its plan on Monday with the first daily $50 million dollar purchase on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The peso fell slightly on Monday and Tuesday, but held at 456.30 pesos per dollar for the subsequent two days.\par \par \uc2 "The biggest impact is the announcement effect. The shock of the announcement is a very strong signal to the market that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government authorities don't want the peso to appreciate nearly as fast as it has been," said Anderson, who left his year-end forecast for the dollar at 450 pesos.\par \par \uc2 RATE SIGNALS AND INFLATION\par \par \uc2 With the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank is sending a message that it will not raise rates despite high current inflation. The message was not lost on the local stock market which rose for five consecutive days.\par \par \uc2 "You're not going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if you're planning on hiking. That would be counterproductive," said Pedro Tuesta, senior analyst with 4CAST in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "They're even indicating that they may cut rates. That reduces the interest rate differential and means the (peso) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should weaken."\par \par \uc2 While Chile has raised its benchmark rate to 6.25 percent to rein in inflation, the U.S. federal Reserve has slashed its fed funds rate to 2.25 percent to stave off recession, which has made the peso a more attractive investment.\par \par \uc2 Following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tuesta said he adjusted his year-end forecast upward to 495 pesos per dollar.\par \par \uc2 A side effect of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is higher inflation.\par \par \uc2 "What's happening is the bank is borrowing pesos and if they're borrowing pesos, they're increasing the money supply and that causes inflation," said Anderson, who mentioned China's and Argentina's big inflation problems.\par \par \uc2 "It can work for a little while but it doesn't work for periods of much longer than a year. Inflation gets away from you real quick," he added.\par \par \uc2 Chile's relatively strong economic growth should give it room to raise rates to control inflation, but the bank is less likely to exercise that option with the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have forgotten about inflation. If you can't increase rates when you have inflation, then you shouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because if you do you're defeating yourself," said Tuesta.\par \par \uc2 While emerging markets are trying desperately to curb the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against dollar because of their dependence on foreign trade, there is little they can do.\par \par \uc2 "At the end of the day, the movements we see in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are going to depend on the United States," said Rodrigo Aravena, economist with the Banchile brokerage, who has held his year-end forecast to 480 pesos per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The problem is not the Chilean peso but the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Take the dollar out of the equation and the Chilean peso really hasn't changed much relative to the euro or the yen. It's only changed relative to the dollar," Anderson said. (Editing by Clive McKeef)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHILE/PESO (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|C|GRO|MTL|SOF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080420e44h000j8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
