\par\par \b \uc2 Chilean farmers protest strong peso, want action   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Rodrigo Martinez   \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 01:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  REQUINOA, Chile, March 27 (Reuters) - Hundreds of farmers and farmhands protested in Chile's agricultural heartland on Thursday to demand authorities take measures to boost their profits in a sector hammered by the strengthening peso.   \par \par \uc2  Farmers say production costs have doubled in the past five years due to a nearly 45 percent appreciation of the peso against the U.S. dollar and sharp rises in fuel, labor and other expenses.   \par \par \uc2  Sector leaders and businessmen joined workers carrying placards and black flags in the farming town of Requinoa, 60 miles (100 km) south of the capital Santiago, vowing more demonstrations to press for government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action.   \par \par \uc2  "We have seen a certain insensitivity on the part of the economic authorities," said Luis Schmidt, president of the National Agricultural Society union. "What we need is a political decision to face up to the issue."   \par \par \uc2  The orderly protest was held by a motorway. Two donkeys stood by the roadside, wearing banners that read "Finance Ministry" and "\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2  Exporters have appealed to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is autonomous, to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the peso to bring down their overheads like labor costs and to make their dollar revenues worth more in peso terms.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jose de Gregorio has warned the bank has not ruled out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has warned \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators not to look for a bailout. But so far the bank has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and has not done so for almost a decade.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso, which trades in a free-float exchange rate, is now worth about 443 to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry has decreed measures to help exporters, such as simplifying taxes, but has deferred to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the issue of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Chile, the world's top copper producer, has seen a boom in global commodities prices boost its trade surplus. Its main agricultural exports are fruit and farmed fish.   \par \par \uc2  RUNAWAY PESO   \par \par \uc2  Chile's peso has strengthened nearly 14 percent against the dollar so far this year to its highest levels in more than a decade on the back of strong copper prices and a widening gap between domestic and U.S. interest rates.   \par \par \uc2  Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised its benchmark rate to 6.25 percent to control inflation.   \par \par \uc2  By contrast, the U.S. Federal Reserve slashed its benchmark federal funds rate last week by three-quarters of a point to 2.25 percent, the lowest since February 2005, to try to jump-start the weak U.S. economy and ease a mortgage crisis.   \par \par \uc2  "We share the need to be very alert to what happens with economic competitiveness and in particular the exchange rate," Finance Minister Andres Velasco said in Santiago, adding the government was considering new economic measures.   \par \par \uc2  Fuel prices, which have shot up amid a drought that has dried up reservoirs and hit hydroelectric power generation, are also hurting the farm sector.   \par \par \uc2  Union leader Schmidt said 45,000 farm workers were fired in 2006 and another 12,000 in 2007 due to falling profitability.   \par \par \uc2  The sector employs about 800,000 people on a permanent basis and 500,000 part time in the country of around 16 million people.   \par \par \uc2  "I came to support my company because, given the fall of the dollar, we risk our company being closed down and losing our jobs," said Maria Castillo, who works at a plant that exports lemons, cherries, pears and apples.  (Editing by Simon Gardner and John O'Callaghan)      \par \par \uc2 CHILE-FARMERS (PIX)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080328e43s00010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
