\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Chile central bank \highlight\uc2  has no peso  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  plan  \b0\par\par\uc2 486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2006\par 17:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds De Gregorio comment on no tax cuts)  \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, May 30 (Reuters) - Chileans can forget about tax cuts in the face of windfall profits from copper, and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the powerful peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely except in extreme cases, officials said.  \par \par \uc2 Jose De Gregorio, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice president, said on Tuesday the institution will stick to a free-floating exchange rate, and urged calm from Chileans who say they are being hurt by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.  \par \par \uc2 Chile's peso, buoyed by soaring prices for No. 1 export copper, has surged in value over the past year -- though it has lost ground recently -- and exporters have said their dollar-denominated profits are being hurt.  \par \par \uc2 "We must not overreact" on the exchange rate, De Gregorio said at a Santiago seminar on Chile's windfall copper profits.  \par \par \uc2 Chile is the world's largest copper producer and, with prices soaring to record highs, government income from mining taxes are expected to hit $3.3 billion this year.  \par \par \uc2 "Not everything is copper-colored," De Gregorio said. "Copper is pretty important, but we mustn't overreact about its impact on the economy."  \par \par \uc2 At the same seminar, Chile Finance Minister Andres Velasco said the government had no plans to cut income tax, even as surging copper revenue fattens public finances.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think that's a path we want to take," Velasco said, adding that the government was already taking other steps to manage the funds.  \par \par \uc2 The government said in April it would hold some copper profits in a special pension reserve fund and some in a new copper fund to save dollars at home or abroad.  \par \par \uc2 "Cutting taxes does not seem to me to be the correct macroeconomic response," to high copper revenues, Velasco said.  \par \par \uc2 The copper profit has helped boost the peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by some 9 percent in the past year, although it has weakened recently, in part because of the government's pledges to invest copper funds abroad.  \par \par \uc2 In the year to date, the peso has lost about 3 percent against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 On the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday, the peso <CHILJ> <CLP=CL> fell 1.27 percent in midday trade to 533.20/533.70 per dollar compared with Monday's close at 526.50/527.00.  \par \par \uc2 "We are in a pretty reasonable situation," said De Gregorio.  \par \par \uc2 "You can't spend it all, or debit a tax cut. A cut in taxes or rise in spending is practically the same to me."  \par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , De Gregorio said such measures were reserved, "for when we are truly convinced of an exchange rate overreaction."  \par \par \uc2 He also said such measures would only be implemented if they were relatively assured of success.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CHILE-PESO (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|U|C|MTL|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060530e25u0019l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
47
