\par\par \b \uc2 Chile stocks slip for a 3rd day, peso retreats  \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 22:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SANTIAGO, Chile, March 2 (Reuters) - Chilean blue-chips slipped on Thursday for a third day as traders took profits, and the peso fell following remarks by outgoing President Ricardo Lagos expressing concern over the dollar's exchange rate versus the peso.  \par \par \uc2 The IPSA index of 40 most-liquid stocks <.IPSA> slipped 0.31 percent to 2,145.72 and the broader IGPA index <.IGPA> fell 0.06 percent to 9,701.10, preliminary closing figures showed.  \par \par \uc2 "What we saw today were portfolio adjustments," said Cesar Perez-Novoa, research manager with the Celfin investment bank. "And with final earnings reports in yesterday, everybody has the information on each company and it's normal to see short-term adjustments."  \par \par \uc2 Wood products manufacturer and forestry company Masisa led declines in the more heavily traded blue-chips. Its stock slumped 3.06 percent to 102.76 pesos per share.  \par \par \uc2 Masisa, controlled by Swiss industrialist Stephan Schmidheiny through Grupo Nueva S.A., reported late on Wednesday that 2005 net profits were off by 50 percent from the previous year.  \par \par \uc2 Stock in industrial conglomerate and wood pulp manufacturer Copec slipped 0.84 percent to 4,800 pesos a share, while its holding company Antar Chile fell 1.27 percent to 6,150 pesos. Copec reported net profit down 29 percent on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Some of the session's losses were offset by gains in the retail sector.  \par \par \uc2 Supermarket chain D&S' stock rose 1.3 percent to 179 pesos a share, while heavily weighted Falabella rose 1.19 percent to 1,533 pesos a share.  \par \par \uc2 On the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Chilean peso <CHILJ> <CLP=CL> retreated 0.42 percent to 519.70/520.20 per dollar, compared with Wednesday's close at 517.50/518.00.  \par \par \uc2 In a radio interview on Thursday morning, Lagos indicated that if the dollar continued falling, the administration of President-elect Michelle Bachelet, who takes office on March 11, may have to work with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to find a way to stop the dollar's slide against the peso.  \par \par \uc2 "President Lagos' statement caused investors to increase dollar holdings and added to market perceptions that the economic team of the new president will promote a stronger dollar," a trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Many exporters have been calling for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to support the dollar. But until now, authorities have maintained a hands-off policy regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 CHILE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -STOCKS|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060302e23200252\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
