\par\par \b \uc2 Chile stocks up in heavy volume, peso at 7-wk high  \b0\par\par\uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2006\par 22:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO, Chile, Feb 21 (Reuters) - Chilean stocks rose in brisk trade on Tuesday, led by electric utility and forestry products companies, while the peso closed at a seven-week high against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2  The IPSA blue-chip index <.IPSA> rose 68 percent to 2,113.95 points while the broader IGPA index <.IGPA> climbed 0.55 percent to 9,550.01, preliminary closing figures showed.  \par \par \uc2  "The IPSA was driven by electric and forestry sectors, and by big volumes," said Cesar Perez-Novoa, research manager with the Celfin investment bank.  \par \par \uc2  Regional energy group Enersis  traded 1.91 percent higher at 125 pesos a share, while energy distributor Endesa  surged 2.74 percent to 535 pesos a share.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the sector was boosted by news of the offer German energy group E.ON  made to acquire Endesa Spain , which is the parent company of Enersis and Endesa.  \par \par \uc2  Endesa Spain said on Tuesday that the offer, which placed the company's value at some $35 billion, was clearly better than the offer by Gas Natural, but did not adequately reflect the value of the company.  \par \par \uc2  Other gainers included wood products manufacturer Masisa , which led the blue-chips as its stock jumped 3.85 percent to 108.50 pesos a share, while shares in forestry and paper firm CMPC  rose 1.04 percent to 13,550 pesos a share.  \par \par \uc2  "The forestry sector has been favored by a rise in the price of wood products especially pulp," said Perez-Novoa.  \par \par \uc2  On the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Chilean peso <CHILJ> <CLP=CL>  advanced 0.81 percent to 518.50/519.00 per dollar compared to Monday's close at 522.70/523.20.  \par \par \uc2  Traders said banks exercised stop-loss orders after the peso broke through the 520-per-dollar resistance level.  \par \par \uc2  "What's happening is that the conditions are there for the peso to continue appreciating. The market understood it that way and went out to stop losses," a trader said.  \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the 93rd time since October on Monday to shore up the flagging dollar, but the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has steadfastly maintained a hands-off policy toward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .       \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 CHILE\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060221e22l001tz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
