\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Chile sees faster economic growth, buoyed by copper.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Antonio de la Jara  \par \uc2 511 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2004\par 16:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 VALPARAISO, Chile, Jan 14 (Reuters) - Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  painted a rosy picture for the economy in 2004, forecasting faster growth and a higher trade surplus, buoyed by an Asia-driven jump in prices of its leading export copper.  \par \par \uc2 In a presentation to the Senate, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Vittorio Corbo raised the forecast range for 2004 economic growth by a half of a percentage point to between 4.5 percent and 5.5 percent. He maintained the bank's estimate of 3.2 percent growth for 2003.  \par \par \uc2 "Since the last report (in September), there have been significant changes in the international arena and that has consolidated the recovery and our main exports," Corbo said in his quarterly report to the Senate in Valparaiso.  \par \par \uc2 The bank sees copper prices averaging $1.00 per pound this year, up from a previous estimate of 83 cents per pound, as China leads an upturn in demand for the metal amid a global economic recovery which would help export-oriented Chile overall.  \par \par \uc2 Chile, the world's No. 1 copper producer and one of the most open economies in Latin America, earns an additional $100 million for every one cent rise in world copper prices.  \par \par \uc2 The copper rally will help boost the South American nation's trade surplus to $4.6 billion this year, the bank said, up more than 30 percent from its previous trade surplus estimate of $3.5 billion. Copper traditionally accounts for 40 percent of Chile's total exports.  \par \par \uc2 Exports will also rise due to a bilateral free trade deal between Chile and the United States, its biggest trade partner, that went into effect Jan. 1, dropping duties on 87 percent of all goods. Chile has a similar accord with the European Union.  \par \par \uc2 FEAR OF DEFLATION  \par \par \uc2 One soft spot in the economy is a threat of deflation coupled with a 20 percent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in 2003 that has sent exporters crying out for \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the peso .  \par \par \uc2 But Corbo, who maintained the bank's 2004 inflation estimate of 2 percent, gave no sign of stepping in to stop the peso's climb.  \par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate has not gone out of orbit ... it's an international phenomenon," Corbo said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a floating exchange rate but the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell or buy dollars if it considers the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be unstable. Its last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was during the financial crisis leading up to the Brazilian presidential elections in 2002.  \par \par \uc2 After weeks of gains, the peso began sliding this week and after Corbo's remarks, it fell at midsession on Wednesday to 577.00/577.50 per dollar from Tuesday's close of 566.00/566.50.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation in 2003 was 1.1 percent, the lowest since 1935 after consumer prices dropped in December for the third straight month.  \par \par \uc2 The undesirably low inflation prompted the bank to slash its benchmark interest rate twice in both December and January to a historic low of 1.75 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040114e01e002tg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
