\par\par \b \uc2  Chile's Peso Ends Stronger On Lack Of US Dollar Demand \b0\par\par\uc2 219 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2002\par 19:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- Chile's peso closed slightly stronger in low-volume trading as dollar demand from U.S. market participants was absent due to the Columbus Day holiday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The peso finished at CLP745.50 a dollar versus Friday's CLP747.20. \par \par \uc2  Over the coming sessions, the peso is likely to trade between CLP740-750, they added. \par \par \uc2  U.S. banks are the Chilean market's main counterparties,  so their absence helped the peso climb, domestic traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Pressure from the low (Brazilian) real and the bombing  in Indonesia limited the recovery," a Santiago trader said. \par \par \uc2  International news will be the main factor influencing the peso this week, with the Brazilian elections and the Middle East crises looming large, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with up to $2 billion and auction another $2 billion in dollar-denominated  bonds after the peso - already at historic lows - began to fall faster. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The first two of these auctions will take place this week, "but they\par won't determine the dollar price" even though they will provide dollar\par liquidity, the Santiago trader said.\par    -By Stephan Kueffner, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8546;\par stephan.kueffner@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1170 1844 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021014dyae000xy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
