\par\par \b \uc2  Americas Stocks Close Lower on War Fears, Wall Street's Slide \b0\par\par\uc2 878 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2002\par 04:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Most major Americas markets closed lower Wednesday as Wall Street's tumble, the increasing chances of war with Iraq and a flurry of profit warnings soured investor sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Selling accelerated on Wall Street late Wednesday as investors pocketed quick profits from the previous session's impressive -- albeit short-lived -- rally. The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 183.18 points, or 2.3%, while the Nasdaq Composite Index gave back 2.2%. \par \par \uc2  Toronto's S&P/TSX Composite Index fell 117.93 points, or 1.9%, to close at 6100.68. Decliners topped advancers 528 to 443 and trading volume was 145.1 million shares, slightly down from Tuesday's total of 147.3 million shares. The S&P/TSX 60 Index closed down 7.97 points, or 2.29%, to 339.68. \par \par \uc2  Nine of the TSE's 10 stock groups fell. \par \par \uc2  The industrial group was the worst hit, falling 3.4%. \par \par \uc2  Railroads posted steep falls after Burlington Northern Santa Fe issued a profit warning related to the lockout of workers at U.S. West Coast ports. Canadian Pacific Railway closed down 2.15 to 27.10 and Canadian National Railway ended 3.01 lower to 58.94. \par \par \uc2  Air Canada fell 0.22 to 4.63 after UBS Warburg cut the stock to hold from buy. Flight-simulator maker CAE Inc. fell 0.59 to 4.01 following its fiscal 2003 profit warning. Conglomerate Bombardier Inc. slipped 0.20 to 4.36 as the grim outlook for the aerospace sector continued to weigh on the company. \par \par \uc2  Mexican stocks also ended mostly lower, dragged down by losses in U.S. markets on what participants called a technical retreat. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's key IPC index closed down 98.95 points, or 1.7%, at 5827.71. Decliners outnumbered gainers 32 to 19, while 10 issues were unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Industrial conglomerate Desc said Wednesday that economic weakness cut its sales between July and September to $507 million, down from $553 million for the same period one year ago. Exports represent more than 40% of Desc's sales. The company makes auto parts, pretochemicals and processed foods. \par \par \uc2  Desc estimated that third-quarter earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization, or Ebitda, fell to $60 million from $79 million in the same quarter of 2001, while operating profit fell to $29 million from $46 million. \par \par \uc2  Desc's C shares slid 7% in thin tradem while its B shares fell 8.1%. \par \par \uc2  Guidance has also come in from Cemex, the world's third-largest cement company. Cemex said nearly two weeks ago that it expects Ebitda of $520 million for the quarter, down from $562 million in the same period last year. Weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Mexico and Venezuela are in part to blame. \par \par \uc2  The company also warned that cash earnings should be 6% lower than the $421 million reported last year and that majority net income for the third quarter will likely be significantly lower than the same period of 2001. \par \par \uc2  Cemex CPO shares fell 4.2%. \par \par \uc2  Mexican companies are expected to begin reporting official results for the quarter in mid-October. \par \par \uc2  Argentine stocks fell with investors retreating amid little fresh news on the government's bid for an International Monetary Fund debt-rollover program. The Merval blue-chip index fell almost one point, or 0.5%, to 392.06. The broad General Index fell 31.25 points, or 0.1%, to 21035.91 points. Seventeen shares gained and 12 declined. But declines in blue chips offset any broader gains. Another 14 shares were unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Argentine President Eduardo Duhalde is pressing the IMF for an agreement permitting the government to roll over maturing loans through December 2003. But the government has been tightlipped on talks so far this week. \par \par \uc2  Steel maker Acindar fell 3%, while oil company Perez Companc fell 3%. \par \par \uc2  In Brazil, despite early hopes for a continuation of Tuesday's rally, share prices dropped under the weight of the heavy U.S. sell-off. The main Sao Paulo index ended 1.9% lower at 8820. \par \par \uc2  "With all the recent uncertainty, the local market was somewhat directionless and got pulled under by international factors," said a trader in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Wednesday's slide came despite attractive local prices that have had local investors forecasting an upturn lately. \par \par \uc2  Brazilian share prices have dropped more than 30% since the beginning of the year amid investor nervousness ahead of the upcoming presidential elections. Monday, shares closed at their lowest since February 1999. \par \par \uc2  Local equities were also hurt Wednesday by a slide in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which dropped about 1.5% during the day despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its favor. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , blue-chip share prices on the Santiago Stock Exchange ended lower, with institutional investors sidelined, showing how fragile any recovery will be. \par \par \uc2  Chile's 40-share blue-chip IPSA index ended down 1.2% at 78.77, down from 79.72 Tuesday, while the broader-based IGPA index ended down 0.4% at 4695.08 from 4716.20. \par \par \uc2  The most affected shares in the sell-off were highly liquid U.S.-traded stocks, particularly utilities and telecommunications companies. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020021003dya3000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20