\par\par \b \uc2  Chile's Peso Calms As Fresh Reasons For Fall Fade \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2002\par 21:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Stephan Kueffner \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- After several plunges in a row, the Chilean peso on Tuesday found some solid footing. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to CLP739.40 to the dollar from CLP748.90 Monday as the market began to fear \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  set record low after record low in the past two weeks, traders now expect it to trade in a CLP720-750 range unless major external pressures such as actual hostilities between the U.S. and Iraq or a further meltdown in the Brazilian real overwhelm domestic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar could rise a little more but these levels aren't sustainable for a long time because the market knows the authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time," said Francisco Montaner, director of online brokerage Lemon Financial. \par \par \uc2  Others also think the big sell-off is over for the time being. \par \par \uc2  "While the uncertainty that has been pressuring the peso remains in place - a possible Iraqi war and elections in Brazil - there has been no concrete event that justifies a greater rise to the dollar," said Cristian Gardeweg, economist at Santander Investment. \par \par \uc2  So far, Chile's government has been more vocal than the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in supporting the peso. President Ricardo Lagos recently criticized peso speculators and Finance Minister Nicolas Eyzaguirre said Monday there were no economic reasons that justified current dollar levels. \par \par \uc2  When the peso was flirting with its previous historic low of CLP718.70, set last October, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Carlos Massad had said the fall was justifiable considering low prices for copper, Chile's main export commodity, and the surge in crude oil prices. \par \par \uc2  While ruling out direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the time, he also said the bank might step in at any time to combat speculation, a stance he reiterated Monday, adding that the bank could move if inflation threatened to rise above an annual 3% due to the peso's devaluation. \par \par \uc2  The statements by Eyzaguirre and Massad helped calm the market since "even if Massad said nothing new and only said that it hasn't given up on its option to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's good to sometimes remind the market of these things," said Santander's Gardeweg. \par \par \uc2  While the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could feasibly buy pesos directly, a more likely course of action would be the issuance of dollar-denominated bonds, as it has done in the past, he added. \par \par \uc2  Indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also stem from major state-owned companies, above all copper giant Codelco and energy company Enap, Lemon's Montaner said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank Could Have Taken Strong Stance Earlier, Some Say\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some economists criticize Massad's position as being too lax.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is far from being a deep market, so\par it needs signals from the authorities to manage uncertainty," said\par economist Patricio Arrau at think tank Gemines.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par When the peso set its previous record low due to the Argentine crisis\par and the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks on the U.S., "a signal that the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had $2 billion to auction new dollar bonds and to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par directly was enough for the market to start correcting itself," he\par added.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reported that as of Sept. 13 foreign reserves\par had risen to $15.12 billion, up 1.2% from $14.94 billion on Aug. 31.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The high level of reserves and low external debt rates would enable\par the bank to easily set aside $2 billion to $3 billion to stabilize the\par exchange market, Arrau said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He added he considered exchange rates around CLP650 to the dollar,\par last seen in June, as fair.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Stephan Kueffner, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8546;\par stephan.kueffner@dowjones.com (Patricia San Juan contributed to this\par report)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020924dy9o0017l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
